     1	# Design: a thesis the ladder refuses is not open (fix 1 of PATH-TO-PASS; $0 design, nothing built)
     2	
     3	**The defect** (census C)
     4	- Step 1 runs over the whole window before the ladder, so a thesis the ladder later refuses stays in Step 1's open-thesis state.
     5	- Later items file their evidence under it and never open a well-formed thesis.
     6	- In the two new draws, capex, DRAM, Groq and the neocloud matters were lost this way.
     7	
     8	**The rule, owned by code (identity, not judgement):** a thesis is open only once the ladder has accepted it.
     9	
    10	**Where it goes:** in Step 1's item loop (`clean-arch/step1/runner.py` `run`), after an answer is checked. Each new thesis of that answer is
    11	laddered at once, with the frozen ladder request (astra-frozen, pass 1, the same thesis and item fields `build_cases` writes).
    12	- **Accepted:** the thesis enters `theses`, which is the next item's open list. Its pass-1 ladder is stored with the trace row.
    13	- **Refused:** the thesis does not enter `theses`.
    14	  - The evidence the answer linked only to it is kept unlinked (D383), so the later-linking step can file it.
    15	  - The refusal and its reason are recorded on the trace row.
    16	- **The pipeline's ladder component reads the stored pass-1 ladders** and runs passes 2 and 3 as now, for D400's checks.
    17	
    18	## State × event
    19	| State | Event | Transition and what is written |
    20	|---|---|---|
    21	| Item answered, new thesis proposed | Ladder accepts | The thesis is added to `theses`; the ladder is stored on the row; the next item sees it |
    22	| Same | Ladder refuses (one refusal note and one resend, stage_a's rule) | Not added; the refusal and its reason are on the row; evidence linked only to it goes unlinked |
    23	| Same | Ladder transport failure after its retries | The row is failed with its reason; the run continues; the thesis is not opened. Recorded beside D449: a format or transport failure at the ladder is not a refusal |
    24	| Same | Crash midway (the row is in flight) | As now: the run stops; a relaunch finds the row in flight |
    25	| A thesis already open | A later item proposes the same bet | As now (Step 1 links to the open thesis); the ladder is not run again |
    26	| Refused thesis | A later item raises the same matter, well formed | Proposed anew, laddered, opened if accepted. The earlier unlinked evidence is found by later linking |
    27	| Configuration changed between days | — | Refused by the launcher's identity check, as now |
    28	| End of the run | — | Every open thesis has a pass-1 ladder; `build_cases` counts the opened theses from the states. Its count rule is unchanged: refused theses never enter a state |
    29	
    30	## Value × reader
    31	- **`theses`, the open state:** read by the next item's request (`open_theses`), by `build_cases` (`opened_count`, from the states) and
    32	  by Step 2 (links). All of them now see accepted theses only.
    33	- **The pass-1 ladder:** stored with the trace row. It is read by the ladder component, which must not re-send pass 1, and by the judge's
    34	  inputs.
    35	- **Evidence linked only to a refused thesis:** unlinked, read by Step 2 and by later linking (D383, D513).
    36	
    37	## New path × old checks
    38	- **`build_cases`'s count check:** it holds, because states contain accepted theses only.
    39	- **The ladder's pass-1 archive:** it moves into the Step 1 folder. The judge's input path changes (`judge-inputs.json` names it).
    40	- **The D400 three-pass checks:** pass 1 is stored at opening, passes 2 and 3 are sent as now, and the fingerprints are unchanged.
    41	- **Cost:** about one ladder call per opened thesis, the same calls the ladder component makes now, moved earlier.
    42	
    43	## Prior decisions
    44	| Decision | What it says | How the design stands |
    45	|---|---|---|
    46	| D383 | A kept statement no thesis takes is kept and linked later | Holds |
    47	| D397 / D436 | A second side of the same bet is one question | Unchanged; the ladder still decides |
    48	| D432 | The no-ladder theses were diagnosed | This acts on their consequence, not their wording |
    49	| D493 | No further Step 1 request change | No request text changes; the loop's control flow does |
    50	| D427 | Clean Step 2 is frozen | Untouched |
    51	| D448 / D449 | The wiring and the redo | The ladder's failure policy is listed above for review |
    52	
    53	**Not decided here:** the ladder's own refusal of a deal's completion and size (fix 3), and D505 (fix 4).
    54	
    55	**The test, after Alex's word:** a cents probe that replays new-2's July–September items over the capex, DRAM and Groq matters with the
    56	rule. Measured: whether a well-formed thesis opens and is accepted for M02, M03, M16 and M25.
     1	# What it would take for the new system to pass the release comparison (written path, D677's condition for reopening)
     2	
     3	**The pass line.** a − b ≥ 5 and a ≥ 2b, under both countings, on the frozen 32 matters. The old system serves 10.
     4	
     5	## The three changes, and what each could add (counted on the 5-draw diagnostic; these are ceilings)
     6	
     7	### A. Stop losing opened questions (mechanism 2)
     8	- **The loss:** 27 of 60 (new-2) and 19 of 60 (new-3) theses get no ladder, most for bundling several periods or outcomes.
     9	- **Where the fix goes:** structural, not in Step 1's wording (D493). When the ladder refuses a thesis for bundling, a splitter writes one
    10	  thesis per period or outcome that meets section 3, and each is laddered.
    11	- **The cheap test:** the 46 stored no-ladder cases. The ladder runs with one worker, so the local cap holds a single reservation.
    12	- **The ceiling:** the new system gains M04 and M17 in both draws, and M19 in new-2, plus possibly M14 and M16. It reaches 5–6 matters.
    13	  With A alone the old system still has 4 to 5 matters only it serves (NVIDIA's own figures, and M31 at counting 2): no winner at
    14	  counting 1 and a loss at counting 2, never a lead.
    15	
    16	### B. Hold the company's core figures (mechanism 1): needs Alex to lift D505
    17	- **The gain:** M06, M10, M11 and M18, which the old system serves, and M07, M09 and M30, which it does not or only in one draw.
    18	- **What it brings b to:** about 0–1.
    19	- **The known side effect** (D490/D491): Vera Rubin product statements filed under revenue. Cents probes on stored states must show the
    20	  product question still opens.
    21	
    22	### C. Open more of the rule-allowed big matters (framing and coverage)
    23	- **The candidates:** 14 matters the new rules allow and the old system misses.
    24	  - Shared quantities: M02, M03, M05, M14, M16, M24.
    25	  - Obligations: M08, M12, M20, M27, M32.
    26	  - Policy: M25, M26, M28.
    27	- **Today:** some are opened but framed beside the analysts' matter (M20 as an amount where the matter is a signing; M25 as review closure
    28	  where the matter is enforcement; M32 in one draw). M05 and M08 are never opened.
    29	- **What a lead needs:**
    30	  - with B, about 5 of these served by the new system alone, reliably in 2 of 3 draws;
    31	  - without B, about 9.
    32	
    33	## The arithmetic
    34	- **A, B and five from C:** a ≈ 5–8, b ≈ 0–1. A lead is possible.
    35	- **A and C without B:** b ≥ 4, so a lead needs a ≥ 9 of 14. Very unlikely.
    36	
    37	## The order and the cost
    38	1. **A** on stored cases: cents.
    39	2. **B** probe on stored states: cents, but only if Alex lifts D505.
    40	3. **C:** a $0 census of why each of the 14 is framed off or never opened, then cents probes.
    41	4. **One full comparison:** about $150 for six draws, run only after 1–3 pass on stored data and only on Alex's word, as it exceeds D439's
    42	   $40.
    43	
    44	## Census C ($0), 2026-11-19: why the 14 rule-allowed matters are lost
    45	- **No new thesis opens in the window.** On the articles behind these matters in 09-07..09-20, new-2 opened 0 new theses, and new-3 the
    46	  same.
    47	- **The evidence is filed under questions the ladder dropped.** Step 1 runs over the whole window before the ladder, so its open-thesis
    48	  state still holds theses the ladder later refuses. It files the window's evidence under them, and so never opens a well-formed one.
    49	  - M02 and M03, five-hyperscaler capex 2026 and 2027: about 25 evidence links per draw go to dropped theses (new-2 clean-26, clean-7,
    50	    clean-5; new-3 clean-29, clean-6, clean-12, clean-4).
    51	  - M25, DOJ and Groq, is even filed under a dropped capex thesis.
    52	  - M16 and M32 are mostly unlinked, or linked to dropped theses.
    53	- **A refusal loses its matter for the rest of the run:** the cascade, a refusal plus a stale open-thesis state.
    54	- **The structural fix is code-owned identity:** a thesis with no ladder is not open. The ladder runs when a thesis opens, and a
    55	  refused thesis leaves Step 1's open-thesis state, so a later item can open a well-formed one.
    56	  - This is an identity rule, not a judgement.
    57	  - In the batch replay it needs the ladder interleaved with Step 1 by day.
    58	
    59	## Check A ($0), 2026-11-19: what the 46 no-ladder theses bundle
    60	Read by Claude, from the stored ladder reasons and theses (new-2's 27; new-3's 19 are the same kinds).
    61	- **(i) Two years in one**, independent annual quantities: capex 2026 and 2027 (M02/M04 and M03/M14), custom-ASIC capacity, used-GPU value,
    62	  build cost per gigawatt, global AI capex. Splitting by year is clean, and D397 is not touched.
    63	- **(ii) Several measures of one quantity:** neocloud capex, OEM server revenue and orders, Gulf, Korea, EU, desktop GPUs. The main
    64	  measure is to be named; this is one bet.
    65	- **(iii) A deal's completion and its size, or signing and closing:** Anthropic (M17) and Hugging Face (M19), Poolside, the Vera CPU's
    66	  shipments and revenue. Under D397 a second side of the same bet is one question, but the frozen ladder refuses anything with no single
    67	  main outcome.
    68	  - This is a contract mismatch between the Step 1 standard and the ladder.
    69	  - The ladder should take the deal by its main outcome (completion), with size as context. That is a change to the ladder, a frozen
    70	    piece.
    71	- **(iv) Off-entity or out of scope:** TSMC's own prices, Huawei, AI labs' revenue, Ooredoo. These are refused rightly; Step 1 should not
    72	  open them.
    73	- **For the key matters:** (i) and (iii) carry them (capex, Anthropic, Hugging Face). (i) is fixable in code and by the cascade fix;
    74	  (iii) needs a new version of the ladder.

