   238	
   239	## 4. Step 1: read the item
   240	
   241	### Input
   242	
   243	Step 1 receives the whole item and every open thesis for the item's company: its identity, question, the outcome it tracks and its
   244	period when known. Items are read one at a time, and each sees the library as the previous item left it. Step 1 does not see the
   245	history of evidence; finding earlier versions of a statement is Step 2's job. The current flow covers one company at a time; finding
   246	the other companies an item affects is future design (section 15).
   247	
   248	### What the model decides
   249	
   250	For each statement an analyst would note, one of four results:
   251	
   252	1. **Evidence for an open thesis.** The statement is linked to every open thesis it informs, with the reason.
   253	2. **A new thesis.** Only for a big matter, by one of the standard's four reasons: an obligation the company takes on, its main
   254	   product's next node, a policy stage, or a checkable shared quantity (D384; Alex: "按金标：只开大事"). Another company's single
   255	   project, partnership or product line is evidence, not a thesis. A new thesis carries the entity (supplied by code), the question,
   256	   the outcome to track and the period when known (Alex, 2026-11-06); a meaningful matter may open without a known decision date when
   257	   later developments can settle it.
   258	3. **A material statement no thesis takes yet.** It is kept in the company's record without links and linked when a thesis that
   259	   covers it opens (D383; Alex: "先存着，有观点再挂").
   260	4. **Nothing.** What the closing check rejects is dropped with its reason, never parked.
   261	
   262	Three of Alex's rules shape the reading:
   263	- **A financial figure is a condition, not a thesis** (D382; Alex: "我觉得财务类型不应该是thesis，而更多的是条件。观点应该是独特的view，而不是喊出来的数字，除非有理由 - 这个理由恰恰才是观点").
   264	  A statement of the company's own useful figures lists them, and code checks each value against a verified passage. Those figures are
   265	  evidence for the judgements of related questions (D505).
   266	- **Target prices and valuation conclusions are not kept**; the estimates they rest on are (D385).
   267	- **Lean cautious, catch the increment** (D243): most of an item is common knowledge and is not kept; new information that can change
   268	  an answer is never missed.
   269	
   270	Step 1 decides in the same reading whether a matter is already tracked; there is no separate thesis-routing stage. That is a choice
   271	of this design, and where it fails it fails at opening (section 8).
   272	
   273	### Output
   274	
   275	One answer per item with two lists: new theses and evidence. Each evidence item carries its source passages (each checked by code
   349	## 6. Step 3: save evidence and links
   350	
   351	Step 3 takes the proposals from Step 1 and the comparison outcomes from Step 2 and writes the resulting research information.
   352	
   353	| Result | What is saved |
   354	|---|---|
   355	| New thesis with supporting evidence | The new thesis and its evidence link; save new evidence or reuse a matching record according to Step 2. |
   356	| New evidence for an existing thesis | The evidence and its link to the thesis. |
   357	| Repeat | Place the incoming record in the existing evidence record's group: add its source and any missing link to the relevant thesis, and keep the incoming record's own text and fields inside the group. Do not rewrite the existing record, and do not create a separate evidence record for the same statement. |
   358	| Material statement no thesis takes | The evidence, kept in the company's record without links (D383). When a thesis opens, Jev is asked of each earlier unlinked group whether it informs the thesis; a yes adds the link (section 13). |
   359	| Revision | The new evidence and its source, linked to the earlier statement as a revision. In the judge's ledger the new record also takes the earlier record's thesis links (D202's rule, kept on the clean path by D424; written by the D392 adapter until Step 3 is joined to production storage). |
   360	
   361	A revision does not erase the earlier statement. On 2026-11-06, Alex chose to keep one evidence record for a repeat and add the new source and any missing thesis link. On 2026-11-08 the Step 2 validation showed that keeping only the earlier text discards details a later report adds, such as a guidance condition; Alex then chose to keep each repeat's own text and source inside the stored record's group, without rewriting the original ("A 继续；跑council loop 开始做"; [Step 2 log](step2-validation-2026-11-07/LOG.md) §7). In the judge's ledger each member of a group is its own evidence record, related to the original as the same claim (D204), and the judge reads each copy with that note; a second or later copy of a statement already in the same request is 31% to 34% of what the judge is shown in the saved clean draws (26% to 31% in production's), and how a judgement weighs copies is not tested further (Step 2 log §17). Multiple articles reporting the same statement do not automatically establish independent original sources. The current production writer stores separate repeat records, so this is a change for the clean path and needs implementation and verification; the existing historical records remain available. Step 3 ends when the theses, evidence and relationships are saved. The grouping library (`step3/library.py`, frozen) holds them; a measurement writer (`step4-2026-11-10/stage_b/ladder_adapter.py`) writes them into the judge's ledger together with the judged questions, processing steps, completed days and closings (D429, D564, D567). Joining Step 3 to production storage is part of gate 5 (section 1.5).
   362	
   363	## 7. Step 4: judge the thesis
   364	
   365	Judgement is separate from reading and saving an item. Saving the revised capital-spending forecast records **what the company said**;
   366	judgement says **what that means for the question**, from all the saved evidence. Alex opened this step's design on 2026-11-10:
   367	"设计新系统自己的“判断”环节" (D396). The full design is [Step 4 design](step4-2026-11-10/DESIGN.md) (version 35); this section states
   368	what it does.
   369	
   370	### Two parts
   371	
   372	1. **The ladder step (Step 4a).** One call per newly opened thesis, after Step 3 has saved the item that opened it and before that
   373	   day's judging. It reads the thesis, the article that opened it, the company and its fiscal-year note, and the list of themes; it
   374	   reads nothing later than the opening article. It writes the judged question, its answer choices and its due day, and lists every
   375	   other part of the thesis as not judged. Model: gpt-6-luna at medium effort as frozen (D426); astra (gpt-6-astra) is the candidate under test (D648, D649, D653).
   376	2. **The judge (Step 4b).** Production's frozen judge (judging-v7, D354), unchanged. Once a day it judges every judged question whose
   377	   evidence changed, citing the evidence it used. On a question's due day production's closing step records the final answer (D567).
   378	
   379	### What the ladder step decides
   380	
   381	- **One judged question per thesis**, the part the thesis's outcome names. Every other part is listed as not judged and shown beside
   382	  the answer (D397). A second side of the same bet is context, not a second question (D436).
   383	- **An event** has at most three outcomes: it happened, it is ruled out, or still open by the due day. Code writes "still open"
   384	  (D409). Ruling out is stated as who decides and what they decide (D416); a proceeding that formally ends without the conclusion
   385	  asked for is ruled out (D420).
   386	- **A figure** has exactly five ranges set against a reference, with the favourable end registered and its reason; code orders and
   387	  labels the ranges (D398, carrying D186). An amount already reported may be the reference, labelled as that and never as the market's
   388	  expectation (D411). Where the texts do not settle it, an amount the company owes or guarantees has the lower end favourable (D413).
   389	- **The owner**: the company, or a theme. A government's permission or ban on a class of product belongs to the theme (D418).
   390	- **The due day**: for an event, a stated date or the last day of the thesis's period (D403); for a year's figure, code sets it to the
   391	  fiscal year-end plus 60 days for a company and the calendar year-end plus 90 days for a theme (D414).
   392	- **A decline** is allowed with its reason: no main part, a rolling twelve months with no publication day (D417), or no theme that
   393	  covers the matter. An answer refused twice by code writes no question (D405). A thesis without a judged question is kept, and the
   394	  links to it are counted, not judged.
   395	
   396	### Where it stands
